artial_weighing_ranges: 2 → value 2.\n\n- load_cell_excitation_voltage: 3 V DC → value 3, unit_symbol \"V\", plus power_supply_type \"DC\"? Actually excitation voltage DC. Keep unit_symbol \"V\" and maybe a note. I'll set value 3, unit \"V\".\n\n- minimum_signal_input_voltage: U_min = 0 mV → attribute \"minimum_signal_input_voltage_umin\", value 0, unit \"mV\".\n\n- minimum_input_voltage_per_verification_scale_interval: 0.45 µV → value 0.45, unit \"µV\". Attribute: \"min_input_voltage_per_verification_scale_interval\".\n\n- minimum_load_cell_resistance: 87 Ω → value 87, unit \"Ω\".\n\n- maximum_load_cell_resistance: 1200 Ω → value 1200, unit \"Ω\".\n\n- fraction_of_maximum_permittable_error: 0.5 (this is p_i in R76) → value 0.5, unit null. Label \"fraction_of_maximum_permitted_error\" or \"apportionment_factor_pi\". R76 uses p_i. I'll use \"fraction_of_maximum_permitted_error_pi\" — keep snake_case: \"fraction_of_maximum_permittable_error\" (spelled \"permissible\" in doc). Use \"fraction_of_maximum_permitted_error\" hmm the doc says \"Fraction of the maximum permissible error\". Canonical: \"fraction_of_maximum_permittible_error\"? Correct spelling: permissible. So \"fraction_of_maximum_permittible\" — no. \"permissible\". fraction_of_maximum_permitted_error? I'll use \"fraction_of_maximum_permitted_error\" — actually canonical should reflect document: \"fraction_of_maximum_permittible_error\" is misspelled. Use \"fraction_of_maximum_permitted_error\" (R76 terminology \"maximum permissible error\" → mpe). I'll use \"fraction_of_maximum_permittible_error\" — no, let me just write \"fraction_of_maximum_permitted_error\". Hmm, both fine. Choose \"fraction_of_maximum_permitted_error\".\n\nActually R76 typically calls it p_i \"fraction of the maximum permissible error\". So label: \"fraction_of_maximum_permitted_error_pi\" — I'll keep simpler: \"fraction_of_maximum_permitted_error\".\n\n- load_cell_connection: \"6-wire (with sense technology), may be configured as 4-wire\" → value string.\n\n- max_cable_length_per_cross_wire_section: 2589 m/mm² — that's an odd value (2589 m/mm²); this is the R76 formula-based limit where cable length/cross-section ratio. value 2589, unit_symbol \"m/mm²\". Footnote about sense technology not used — the parenthetical is more of a note than footnote. Could add footnote? The note: \"(if sense technology is not used, load cells connect directly without junction box)\". I could record footnote_markers? There's no marker number. I'll include the parenthetical as... maybe omit or include as part of a separate note. Keep the characteristic value 2589 m/mm² and maybe include the condition in description. I'll add footnote_markers: [] and maybe record the note as a footnote-like? Rule: footnotes extracted from definitions with markers. No marker here. I'll skip.\n\n- temperature_range: 0 °C to +40 °C → value {min: 0, max: 40}, unit_symbol \"°C\".\n\n- power_supply: 100–240 V AC 50/60 Hz or 3.6 V DC battery. Split per rule 3:\n  - power_supply_voltage: {min: 100, max: 240}, unit \"V\"\n  - power_supply_frequency: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit \"Hz\"\n  - power_supply_type: \"AC\"\n  - battery_voltage: 3.6, unit \"V\" (alternative power supply). Label \"battery_supply_voltage\" value 3.6 unit \"V\".\n\n- software_identification: \"Version V2.xx (xx = 01…99)\" → value string \"V2.xx (xx = 01…99)\". Could be \"V2.01–V2.99\". Keep string.\n\nTest reports: NMi-2561497-01, date 4 May 2021 (2021-05-04), pages 53, role type_evaluation_report.\n\nRevision history: none. Footnotes: none.\n\nmodel_family: not applicable (single indicator). Omit.\n\nmatrix_tables: none.\n\nConfig-level: The 6-wire vs 4-wire configuration could be a config axis, but keep simple — the connection characteristic is a string value at type level.\n\nCertificate fields:\n- number: \"R76/2006-A-NL1-21.15\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"2561497\"\n- page_total: not given (only page 2 reviewed). Omit.\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: not given. Omit.\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"? The cert number includes NL1 which is the issuer ID. Hmm — could set oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\". It's plausibly derived from the cert number. I'll include it as \"NL1\".\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: \"NMi\" — the doc says \"(NMi)\". Full name NMi Certin B.V. but don't fabricate. Use \"NMi\".\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nRecommendation: id \"R76\", edition 2006. The cert number R76/2006 → edition year 2006. Schema says edition (int). 2006 as int. accuracy_classes: [\"III\", \"IIII\"].\n\nCategory: \"Indicator\".\n\ntype_designations: none mentioned — omit.\n\nmodule_designation: not applicable? For an indicator, module designation maybe \"Not applicable\"? The schema lists module_designation for e.g. load cells. Omit.\n\nLet me also record \"min_input_voltage_per_verification_scale_interval\" — value 0.45 µV. µV unit symbol: \"µV\".\n\nMax scale intervals: labels: \"max_scale_intervals\" with config axis single vs multi? Could use config_level with axis \"interval_type\": values: condition \"single interval\" → 6000; condition \"multi-interval (per partial weighing range)\" → 3000. That fits config_level nicely. Or type_level with two separate attributes. Either works. I'll use two type_level attributes: \"max_scale_intervals_single_interval\" and \"max_scale_intervals_multi_interval_per_partial_range\". Or use config_level. I'll use config_level:\n\nconfig_level: [{attribute: \"max_scale_intervals\", axis: \"weighing_range_type\", values: [{condition: \"single interval\", value: {min:null,max:6000}, unit_symbol: null}, {condition: \"multi-interval, per partial weighing range\", value: {min:null,max:3000}}]}]\n\nHmm, but the structured value shape in config_level: {condition, value, unit_symbol} per rule 5. The value itself should be structured? Rule 2 says every characteristic value must be object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers. For config_level, schema shows values[]: {condition, value}. I'll make value a structured object too: {\"value\": {\"min\":null,\"max\":6000}, \"unit_symbol\": null}. Actually simpler: since n ≤ 6000 is a max bound, value: {min: null, max: 6000}. Include unit_symbol null.\n\nActually maybe simpler to keep them as type_level attributes with distinct canonical labels. Both are fine. I'll go with config_level to demonstrate proper usage since the axis is genuinely \"single vs multi-interval\". Hmm, but simpler and safer for downstream: type_level. The rules emphasize matrices per model; here it's configuration. Rule 5 explicitly gives this kind of case. I'll use config_level.\n\nAlso load_cell_connection 6-wire vs 4-wire configurable — could be config but it's stated as one capability; keep as string value at type_level.\n\nLet me draft JSON:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R76/2006-A-NL1-21.15\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"2561497\",\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Indicator\",\n    \"module_designation\": null,\n    \"description\": \"Weighing indicator approved for single-interval or multi-interval (max. 2 partial ranges) use, with 6-wire (sense) load cell connection configurable as 4-wire.\"\n  },\n  ...\n}\n\nHmm, description: \"free-text description if present\" — not really present; the summary says \"This page specifies the technical and metrological limits...\" I could omit description. Rule 8 don't fabricate — a description synthesized is borderline. I'll omit description or provide short factual one. Safer: omit.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level:\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"III or IIII\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null}\n- weighing_range_option: {value: \"Single interval or Multi-interval\"}\n- load_cell_excitation_voltage: {value: 3, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- min_signal_input_voltage_umin: {value: 0, unit_symbol: \"mV\"}\n- min_input_voltage_per_verification_scale_interval: {value: 0.45, unit_symbol: \"µV\"}\n- min_load_cell_resistance: {value: 87, unit_symbol: \"Ω\"}\n- max_load_cell_resistance: {value: 1200, unit_symbol: \"Ω\"}\n- fraction_of_maximum_permitted_error: {value: 0.5}\n- load_cell_connection: {value: \"6-wire (with sense technology), may be configured as 4-wire\"}\n- max_cable_length_per_cross_section: {value: 2589, unit_symbol: \"m/mm²\"} — label: \"max_cable_length_per_cross_wire_section\". Note the condition.\n- temperature_range: {value: {min: 0, max: 40}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- power_supply_voltage: {value: {min: 100, max: 240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- power_supply_frequency: {value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}\n- power_supply_type: {value: \"AC\"}\n- battery_supply_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- software_identification: {value: \"V2.xx (xx = 01…99)\"}\n\nconfig_level:\n- attribute \"max_scale_intervals\", axis \"weighing_range_type\": values: [{condition \"single interval (one weighing range)\", value {min null max 6000}}, {condition \"multi-interval per partial weighing range\", value {min null max 3000}}]\n- maybe also max_number_of_weighing_ranges at type_level: {value: 2}.\n\nFor the values in config_level, per schema: values[]: {condition, value}. I'll include unit_symbol as null or omit. I'll include unit_symbol: null for consistency.\n\ntest_reports: [{id: \"NMi-2561497-01\", date: \"2021-05-04\", pages: 53, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}]\n\ndate format: \"4 May 2021\" → \"2021-05-04\".\n\nRecommendation: {id: \"R76\", edition: 2006, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [\"III\", \"IIII\"]}.\n\nAccuracy class value string: \"(III) or (IIII)\". I'll write value: \"III or IIII\".\n\nSoftware note about (i) key — minor; could add as note in description but omit.\n\nDouble-check numbers: 2589 m/mm² — keep as-is even though odd; it's from the doc (it's actually 2589 m/mm² limit per R76 formula 2.5 m/mm²... whatever, 